<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Princeton University</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1746">1746</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> · Also known as College of New Jersey</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Princeton University is a private university in Princeton, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-jersey">New Jersey</a>, chartered in 1746 as the College of New Jersey, the fourth college founded in British <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-america">North America</a>. It moved to Princeton in 1756, where its Nassau Hall briefly housed the Congress of the Confederation in 1783. It took its present name in 1896.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/princeton-university.jpg" alt="Princeton University" width="320" height="409" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Original author unknown. The shield was described in an 1896 minutes of the meeting · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Princeton_seal.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The institution began in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elizabeth-new-jersey">Elizabeth, New Jersey</a>, in 1746 as the College of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-jersey">New Jersey</a>, one of the nine colonial colleges established before the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/american-revolution">American Revolution</a>. Its formative years were shaped by the Log College, a seminary founded by the Reverend William Tennent at Neshaminy, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pennsylvania">Pennsylvania</a>, in about 1726. No legal connection existed, but many of its pupils and adherents later supported the college; Princeton historians reject the older view that it was the college's predecessor.</p><p>After the death of President Jonathan Dickinson the college moved in 1747 to Newark, where the parsonage of his successor Aaron Burr Sr. stood. Governor Jonathan Belcher granted a second charter on 14 September 1748 after the first was disputed by Anglicans, and gave the college his library of 474 volumes.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Nassau Hall, begun in 1754 and finished in 1756, was the first seat of the New Jersey Legislature in 1776 and was involved in the Battle of Princeton in 1777. The Congress of the Confederation met there from 30 June to 4 November 1783. A fire gutted its interior in 1802, and a large student riot in 1807 led to the resignation of President Samuel Stanhope Smith.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/woodrow-wilson">Woodrow Wilson</a>, an alumnus and professor, became the 13th president and introduced the preceptorial system in 1905, adding small-group teaching to lectures. In 1906 the university became nonsectarian. Wilson kept the college closed to Black students. The college had officially become a university in 1896. After 1945 it desegregated and expanded its research, and it explicitly prohibited the admission of women from its founding until 1969.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>Undergraduates complete independent work, including a senior thesis, and first- and second-year students live in one of seven residential colleges. Customs include the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/reunions">Reunions</a> parade of alumni, called the P-rade, and the belief that a student who leaves through the FitzRandolph Gates before graduating will not graduate. Alumni include the presidents <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/james-madison">James Madison</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/woodrow-wilson">Woodrow Wilson</a> and the vice presidents Aaron Burr, George M. Dallas and John Breckinridge. Albert Einstein, who belonged to the Institute for Advanced Study, lectured and visited often and became associated with the university.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Princeton University” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Samuel Davies, later president of <b>Princeton University</b>, preached to enslaved people in unusually large numbers in Virginia.</blockquote><a href="/movement/first-great-awakening">First Great Awakening · People</a></li><li><blockquote>Its first published version appeared as &quot;Levee Song&quot; in Carmina Princetonia, a book of <b>Princeton University</b> songs, in 1894, and its earliest known recording was made by the Shannon Quartet, released by Victor Records in 1923.</blockquote><a href="/songs/ive-been-working-on-the-railroad">I've Been Working on the Railroad</a></li><li><blockquote>Diverse Puritan, Quaker, Dutch and Scottish settlers farmed grain, and the colleges that became <b>Princeton</b> and Rutgers were founded in 1746 and 1766.</blockquote><a href="/state/province-of-new-jersey">Province of New Jersey</a></li></ul></section>
